{"name":"Pine Mountain","hex":"#5c685e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Pine Mountain","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A number of mountains in the United States.","A locality and a large monolith in the Shire of Towong, north eastern Victoria, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Pine_Mountain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pine Mountain (Victoria)","blocks":[{"text":"Pine Mountain is a gigantic monolith, said to be 1.5 times bigger than Uluru, situated in the Burrowa-Pine Mountain National Park in Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Looking_towards_the_summit.JPG/330px-Looking_towards_the_summit.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_Mountain_(Victoria)","wikidata":"Q7195277"},{"title":"Pine Mountain Wilderness","blocks":[{"text":"Pine Mountain Wilderness is a protected wilderness area managed by the Prescott National Forest in the U.S. state of Arizona. Pine Mountain is the high point of the Verde River Rim at 6,814 feet and provides an excellent viewing to the northeast of the Wild and Scenic Verde while surrounded by ponderosa pine and Douglas fir."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_Mountain_Wilderness","wikidata":"Q27974568"},{"title":"Pine","blocks":[{"text":"Pines ) are a genus of conifers in the family Pinaceae. Pinus is the sole genus in the subfamily Pinoideae. The species are trees or shrubs with their leaves in bunches, usually of 2 to 5 needles. The seeds are carried on woody cones, with two seeds to each cone scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Pinus_jeffreyi_at_Lassen_Volcanic_National_Park.jpg/330px-Pinus_jeffreyi_at_Lassen_Volcanic_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine","wikidata":"Q12024"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Western_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg/500px-Western_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Western_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg/1280px-Western_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Western_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg/500px-Western_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Western side of Pine Mountain (summit not visible)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWestern_Side_of_Pine_Mountain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Pinus_range.png/500px-Pinus_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Pinus_range.png/1280px-Pinus_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"htt